WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.880
Welcome back to another episode&nbsp;
of Vintage Egyptologist. Today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.560
I'm going to be talking about historical fiction.&nbsp;
Now this was one of my favorite genres of fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.640
when I was growing up, and I still&nbsp;
love a good historical fiction novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.320
but what's really remarkable - I mean this&nbsp;
is just amazing, even though I've written a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:31.120
book about the topic and I know this so well&nbsp;
the ancient Egyptians had historical fiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:49.920
The year is 1560 BCE. This is what Egyptologists&nbsp;
call part of the Second Intermediate Period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:57.280
which is roughly 1650-1550. This is the time&nbsp;
between the Middle Kingdom where you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:04.320
powerful centralized kingship and the New Kingdom&nbsp;
where again Upper and Lower Egypt are unified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.720
under a single pharaoh however in 1560 there&nbsp;
is a dynasty ruling in the south this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:20.960
17th Theben dynasty so its capital is ancient&nbsp;
Waset in the north you have the 15th dynasty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:28.080
who were a series of Hyksos kings now Hyksos is&nbsp;
a Greek term that comes from ancient Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:36.800
heka khasut that means "ruler of the foreign&nbsp;
lands," so the 15th dynasty are kings that are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:44.960
Egyptian and for any ancient Egyptian to have&nbsp;
part of the Nile Valley ruled by a foreign king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:53.760
is anathema. So the story that is set in this&nbsp;
time period so it was written around 1250 BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:01.920
but set in 1560 BCE and the modern title&nbsp;
that we give it is the Quarrels of Apepi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:09.200
and Sequenere. Apepi is a Hyksos king often&nbsp;
spelled out Apophis in modern histories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:16.640
but this is really misleading because Apophis&nbsp;
is also the Greek term that we tend to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:24.800
for the chaos serpent Apep; so Apepi the Hyksos&nbsp;
king and Apep the chaos serpent sound really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.840
similar to us but it's actually spelled&nbsp;
with two different signs at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.680
in ancient Egyptian so it doesn't seem like&nbsp;
that's a comparison that they're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:42.400
now king Seqenenre is a king of the 17th&nbsp;
dynasty and the way this story begins is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:50.560
Seqenenre and Apepi are having a quarrel not&nbsp;
in terms of a fully fledged military conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:57.680
that is yet to come between the 17th and 15th&nbsp;
dynasties but instead they're having a battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.320
by letter. So I'm going to read here from the&nbsp;
translation that I published in 2013 in my book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.520
imagining the past historical fiction in new&nbsp;
kingdom Egypt and this is how the story starts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:18.320
Then it happened that the land of Egypt was in a&nbsp;
state of pestilence with no legitimate lord living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:25.280
prosperous and healthy as king at that time.&nbsp;
At the same time, it happened that as for King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.360
Seqenenre, he was the sovereign of the southern&nbsp;
city. So that's the reference to ancient Waset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:39.040
Greek Thebes, modern Luxor. Pestilence was in the&nbsp;
city of Re, (That's a reference to Heliopolis)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:46.000
because of them (referencing here the Hyksos),&nbsp;
while the ruler Apepi was in Hut-waret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:53.200
(and that was the capital of the 15th Hyksos&nbsp;
dynasty), and the entire land was controlled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:04:00.240
for him for Apepi bearing their taxes - the&nbsp;
north as well bearing all the good produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.560
of the Delta. So what this is telling us is&nbsp;
that Apepi, the Hyksos king, is essentially in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:15.520
economic control of Egypt even though Seqenenre&nbsp;
is king in the south. Then king Apepi made Seth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:24.160
as lord. This is Seth the god of chaos, the god&nbsp;
who fights with Horus to be king of Egypt. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:31.360
he (meaning Apepi) did not work for any other&nbsp;
god who was in this entire land except Seth;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:39.040
and he built a temple of fine workmanship for&nbsp;
eternity beside the domain of king Apepi; and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.920
he appeared each day in order to make sacrifices,&nbsp;
doing it daily for set while the officials carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:53.520
wreaths. So what this is essentially saying is&nbsp;
that king Apepi is not only not a legitimate king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:05:03.200
of Egypt, but he's committing a religious sin&nbsp;
by offering only to the god Seth and doing so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:11.440
as is done in the temple of Re-Horakhty at&nbsp;
Heliopolis. So he's only worshipping Seth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:18.960
and he's not worshipping him properly. Meanwhile-&nbsp;
and this is where the action really starts - Apepi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:27.520
desired to send an offensive message to the king&nbsp;
Seqenenre, the sovereign of the southern city. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.240
after many days following this - and unfortunately&nbsp;
the text really breaks up here so the next part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:42.000
the story is that Apepi's message reaches Luxor&nbsp;
and it says that his messenger was taken into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:49.200
presence of the sovereign of the southern city&nbsp;
so that's Seqnenere; then One (king Seqenenre)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:55.520
said to Apepi's messenger quote "Why have you been&nbsp;
sent to the southern city? Why have you reached me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.760
with these journeys?" Then the messenger said to&nbsp;
him, "It is king Apepi who sends to you saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:11.280
quote 'Expel the hippopotami from the swamp that&nbsp;
is on the eastern waters of the city, because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:18.800
(talking about the hippopotami) do not allow that&nbsp;
sleep come to me day or night because their noise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:27.440
is in my ear!'" Then the sovereign of the southern&nbsp;
city was surprised for a long moment being unable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:34.640
to respond to the messenger of king Apepi. And&nbsp;
I think we're a little surprised too right. 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:44.320
miles separates Luxor from Avaris, Waset from&nbsp;
Hutwaret, so how are hippopotami 500 miles away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:50.960
keeping Apepi awake? And this is really the crux&nbsp;
of the story. So we know that Apepi worshipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:57.840
Seth to the exclusion of all of the gods and&nbsp;
we know that he is in conflict with Seqenenre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.680
but he sends this message complaining of the&nbsp;
noisiness of the hippopotami and what I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:11.200
is going on here - because the hippopotamus can&nbsp;
be a representation,an animal manifestation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:19.280
of the god Seth is that even though Apepi, the&nbsp;
foreign king, worships Seth, and even though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:27.120
thinks Seth is on his side, Seth with these&nbsp;
noisy hippopotami is actually getting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:34.960
at the foreign king. Unfortunately, the one&nbsp;
papyrus copy we have of this story papyrus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:42.720
Sallier I, which is in the British Museum, which&nbsp;
was copied in year 10 of the reign of Merneptah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:49.120
by a scribe named Pentaweret, seems to have had&nbsp;
other things on his mind by the time he gets to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.800
the end of the story. So it's rather broken up and&nbsp;
then it says that: The sovereign of the southern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.640
city (so this is king Seqenenre again) has his&nbsp;
officials summoned as well as every commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:07.600
and he repeated to them every communication about&nbsp;
which king Apepi sent to him. And they were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:14.800
together quiet for a long time, not knowing how to&nbsp;
respond to him good or bad. Then king Apepi sent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:23.680
And that's it - the text breaks off, but the&nbsp;
papyrus is not actually broken. Instead what seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:31.600
to happen is that Pentaweret has copied enough of&nbsp;
the story that now he moves on to an instruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:40.640
of letter writing. So i think what Pentawaret&nbsp;
is doing is he's promoting scribes. He's saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:46.640
that there are these two kings who are quarreling&nbsp;
but what's most important are the scribes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.680
are writing the letters back and forth the&nbsp;
next part of the British Museum papyrus is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:59.120
rather boring manual of letter writing, so I&nbsp;
think Pentawaret copied this fascinating story the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:06.160
Quarrel of Apepi and Seqenenre to get his scribal&nbsp;
students interested in what's going to come next.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.640
So because we only have one copy of the story, and&nbsp;
it doesn't preserve the ending we don't know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:20.880
happens. We do not know whether in the story Apepi&nbsp;
and Seqenenre actually have a pitched military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:29.360
conflict or who gains the upper hand however we&nbsp;
do know historically what actually happened a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:37.360
pepe continued to be king of the Hyksos until the&nbsp;
reign of Kamose, another seventeenth dynasty king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:44.160
King Seqenenre of Thebes met a very different&nbsp;
fate. We know from his mummy that was discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:51.920
in the Deir el-Bahri cachette of royal mummies&nbsp;
that he died in battle. And we know that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.920 --> 00:10:00.720
there's a Hyksos battle axe shaped hole in his&nbsp;
skull as well as a number of other injuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:09.520
so in the end he died fighting the Hyksso almost&nbsp;
certainly king Apepi, but through the medium of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:15.920
historical fiction the Egyptians were able&nbsp;
to recast this complex time in their history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:23.360
as a narrative of victory and triumph and&nbsp;
ultimately the centrality of scribes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:30.160
literacy in ancient Egypt. And if the readers of&nbsp;
the story did not know a little bit about history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.240
it simply wouldn't have been fun to&nbsp;
read just like historical fiction today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:41.840
so now you've heard all that we have of the&nbsp;
story of the Quarrels of Apepi and Seqenenre, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:47.760
of the most important works of ancient Egyptian&nbsp;
historical fiction. In future episodes, I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:55.680
to introduce you to three additional stories next&nbsp;
up is the Capture of Joppa and ancient Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:02.240
soldiers hiding in baskets to capture the city.&nbsp;
Thank you for tuning in - please don't forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:13.840
to subscribe and there are lots of notes below&nbsp;
if you want to learn more about these stories.

